        "title": "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "9ff245bd-f333-56ba-a6dd-63108fec7de8",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & -7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -5 & 9 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 & -9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 & -3 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & -7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -5 & 9 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 & -9 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are in row echelon form.",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
