      "question": {
        ":question/id": "bb514519-3f32-50b6-9abc-438523d7745b",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -8 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the first row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -8 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form.",
